    {
      "id": "28a69a8e-8d38-5880-8cba-85257c524b17",
      "block_id": "1f292d4a-41a4-4977-9684-7c8d560b9f91",
      "input_default": {
        "model": "claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929",
        "retry": 3,
        "prompt": "You are writing a win-back email to someone who has gone quiet.\n\nWho they are: {{contact}}\nWhat happened, and why they may have gone quiet: {{history}}\nWhat we can offer them now: {{offer}}\nSigned by: {{sender}}\n\nReturn three things, in this order.\n\n## Five subject lines\n\nEach one under six words, lowercase, describing what is inside. Mark the one you\nwould send and say why in a single line.\n\n## The email\n\nSix sentences at most, in this order: what they did last and when, one honest\nsentence acknowledging the gap without apologising for existing, what has changed\nthat is relevant to them specifically, the offer stated plainly with its\ncondition, a question answerable in one line, and an easy way out.\n\nNo greeting beyond their first name. No \"we miss you\". No guilt, no false\nscarcity, no discount unless the offer given above contains one. Write it so the\nrecipient could forward it to a colleague without embarrassment.\n\n## The follow-up plan\n\nThree touches at most, then stop and mark them dormant. For each: how many days\nlater, what new thing it says, and the condition that cancels the rest. A reply\nof \"not now\" ends the sequence \u2014 record the reason and any date they suggest.\n\nNever invent a purchase history, a usage statistic, a previous conversation or a\nresult you cannot name the customer for. Where you need a fact you have not been\ngiven, leave a marked gap in square brackets and list what is missing at the\nend.",
        "sys_prompt": "You are a lifecycle email specialist. You write plainly, you never invent customer data, and you always leave the recipient an easy way out.",
        "prompt_values": {}
      },
